# packet store goes empty incorrectly

- Source URL: https://github.com/SpiNNakerManchester/spio/issues/39

Thanks to Will Toms, University of Manchester, for spotting and diagnosing this bug.

The packet store read pointer **br** and the next packet sequence number **bpkt_seq** are **not** updated in the same clock cycle. Instead they are updated in consecutive ones. This opens a one clock cycle window in which the arrival of an **ack** for the very last  packet to be sent can update the ack pointer **ba** incorrectly. The incorrect update manifests only if **br** was updated exactly the cycle before the arrival of the **ack**.

The consequence is that the next 8 packets to enter the packet store will have already been identified incorrectly as ack'd and, thus, can be overwritten by new packets even before they are transmitted. The observable symptom is that up to 8 packets are "thrown away", *i. e.*, not transmitted.

There is no reason for this update misalignment. Therefore the simplest correct solution is to delay the **br** update one clock cycle so that it coincides with the **bpkt_seq** update.




